WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.040
Today cryptography is the ultimate line of&nbsp;
defense that protects our data. But it's based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:12.440
an assumption that it cannot be broken by classical&nbsp;
computers. In the near future, quantum computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:18.560
may be able to crack these encryption algorithms.&nbsp;
That's why every organizational leader needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:24.342
to understand the risk to their data and the&nbsp;
solutions that will make them quantum safe.

00:00:24.342 --> 00:00:25.447
Let's get to it. 

00:00:25.447 --> 00:00:31.320
What bad actors and cyber criminals&nbsp;
can do once quantum computers mature? First&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:38.000
thing they can do is fraudulent authentication,&nbsp;
which means they can get access to our data--the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.200
secure data that we have in our applications, our&nbsp;
systems, and our databases. Second thing they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:50.480
do is forge signatures, which means they can fake&nbsp;
the records, fake the audits, and also can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:57.080
the fake identity and tamper [with] our blockchain&nbsp;
assets as well. Third thing they can do is harvest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:01.680
now/decrypt later, which is very, very important&nbsp;
for us today. We are understanding that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:08.160
cannot steal and decrypt the data today, but they&nbsp;
will be sitting on it until the quantum computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:14.400
mature and they will be able to decrypt that and&nbsp;
find out the crown jewels from your enterprise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:20.360
The first step is to discover. Discover your&nbsp;
cryptography means you need to identify the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:26.400
cryptography-relevant artifacts in your business&nbsp;
applications--source code as well as object code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.760
What this means is that brings you the visibility&nbsp;
where the cryptography function library's

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:38.160
methods are being used. Once you have them,&nbsp;
you need to also find out the dependencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:44.760
across all of these artifacts and then create a&nbsp;
single source of truth. A similar concept like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.520
in a supply chain inventory called SBOM (software&nbsp;
bill of materials). You need to have the concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:55.760
called cryptography bill of materials (CBOM). IBM&nbsp;
has been helping bringing that as a standard as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:04.360
part of the CycloneDX. And this capability can bring&nbsp;
you a static view of your business applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:10.680
environment. Once you have this, the next step&nbsp;
is observe. Observe your cryptography means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:16.440
bringing the dynamic view along with static&nbsp;
view. Often many organizations have either one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:23.240
or the other, but you need to have both. And&nbsp;
as part of bringing both of these together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:30.240
you need to have a full inventory of your&nbsp;
cryptography assets from network perspective as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.920
well as your applications' perspective. And then&nbsp;
you need to understand how these key exchanges are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:42.240
happening from cryptography perspective like TLS,&nbsp;
SSL from your network environments. And then once&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.720
you get the full visibility of that inventory,&nbsp;
you need to prioritize where your cryptography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:54.960
relevant artifacts are important based on&nbsp;
your cryptography posture or your regulatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:02.600
compliance requirements, and then create&nbsp;
that list. And once you have the prioritized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:10.120
inventory of your cryptography assets, you need&nbsp;
to transform the cryptography by implementing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:16.000
and applying the quantum safe algorithms, or&nbsp;
encryption algorithms, or new certificates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.480
or key lifecycle management capabilities that are&nbsp;
quantum safe. By doing this, you need to make sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:28.360
that you are also following a process that you&nbsp;
can achieve crypto-agility. What we mean by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:36.040
crypto-agility is that [is] how can you reduce the&nbsp;
burden on development as well as the operational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:42.880
environment so that it's not disrupting your&nbsp;
existing systems and applications--and rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:51.120
giving you an ability to move from old algorithms&nbsp;
to new algorithms seamlessly, which means you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:57.240
have crypto-agility as a service capabilities,&nbsp;
starting from encryption, key lifecycle management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:01.920
and certificate management capabilities that&nbsp;
would be quantum safe. And whenever you need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:08.040
them in your business applications, you can simply&nbsp;
make an API call when you need a new encryption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:14.960
when you need a new certificate, when you need a&nbsp;
new key in general. So this helps you bring a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:23.760
capability across Discover, Observe and Transform.&nbsp;
Now you understand the three key steps. What tools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:31.360
should we use to get to your journey to quantum&nbsp;
safe? IBM Quantum Safe Explorer helps you discover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:38.680
your cryptography by scanning the source code and&nbsp;
object code of your enterprise applications. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:43.920
it can create and generate the CBOM, what we&nbsp;
call the cryptography bill of materials. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:51.080
gives you the static view of your IT enterprise&nbsp;
applications. IBM Quantum Safe Advisor can help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:57.400
you bring both the static as well as the&nbsp;
dynamic view of your cryptography assets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:04.920
and also prioritize them based on the cryptography&nbsp;
posture in reference to your compliance and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:12.840
vulnerabilities. IBM Quantum Safe Remediator&nbsp;
is a capability that can bring and allow you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:18.560
to remediate with quantum safe algorithms,&nbsp;
quantum safe key management capabilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:25.960
as well as the certificate management capabilities&nbsp;
that can allow you to be crypto-agile. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:31.640
are a set of remediation patterns that are part&nbsp;
of Remediator that can enable you based on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:38.520
requirements in terms of VPN, in terms of&nbsp;
proxy, in terms of TLS connections. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.920
of those remediation patterns are based on&nbsp;
the best practices that we know as of now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:48.760
based on our current client needs. IBM Quantum&nbsp;
Safe can help any organization not only prepare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:55.160
for the post quantum cybersecurity landscape, but&nbsp;
also improve and maintain their cyber security&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.160 --> 00:06:07.640
hygiene overall. How your organization can prepare&nbsp;
